Now the suggestion:  How about a new character, the alchemist?  Their
specialty would be potions.  Starting inventory would include 3 random
potions, 3 uncursed potions of water, a sack of chemicals, and an
alchemist's kit.  Alchemists start with intrinsic poison resistance.
Starting armor would be a lab coat, gloves (latex?), and safety
glasses; starting weapon would be an X-Acto knife.  And a few candy
bars for food.

The sack of chemicals would have a limited number of charges, like a
bag of tricks.  By dipping the sack into a potion of water, the
alchemist could create any already identified potion (each dip uses
one charge of the sack).  The alchemist could try to create an
unidentified potion, but there would be some chance of failure.  For
example, a potion of confusion should be easy to make, but a potion of
extra healing or gain level difficult if not identified.  Potions of
water should become more common, or quaffed potions should leave
flasks which can be filled from fountains and sinks.

The alchemist's kit would identify potions if applied to them, though
it wouldn't tell if they're cursed or blessed.  It could also be used
to test food to see if it is rotten or poisonous.

All Mad Scientists carry sacks of chemicals.  A few even carry
alchemist's kits.

One of the major goals of alchemy was to find the Philosopher's
Stone--a stone that would turn lead into gold.  So a new gem would be
added, gold in color, that when dropped on a pile of rocks would turn
the rocks into gold (something like 1 or 2 gp per rock).  But beware
the cursed version--the Midas Stone!  If you pick this up, anything
you touch turns to gold.  That won't hurt armor or weapons, but it
would be quite difficult to eat anything.  Items in your pack would be
unaffected until you tried to use them.  Wielded weapons and worn
armor/rings/blindfolds would be affected immediately.  Once uncursed,
the Midas Stone would behave just like the Philosopher's Stone.

